**Definition 13.4 (Publish and actual Call rights).** Publish serializes
a fixed tagged result tuple with Frame and Cat. It measures and copies
that binary word from its actual bank field into a fresh port-39 block,
using Base, a dry SegmentCount, real SegmentCopy, F and seal, and the
complete descriptor creation of Definition 11.3. Payload is one word,
so there are no inter-field sep cells inside it. The same expansion
writes immutable cover and package archive records. Archive kind tags
are actual written bits. Numeric package/cover IDs are the list-count
words, copied into the published payload.

For cross-owner delivery choose the predeclared consumer $C$, mailbox
port 48 and receiver port 40. The client is the original frame actor
$C=A$; installation transfers the unused consumer receiver-port token
to that client by one actual TOK. Original source-frame deliveries still
use their actor archive on 24–31. Service payloads are not interleaved
into that original row stream. Before each stage, measure the arena Base,
construct its actual length argument, execute the complete initialized
Allocate, and write its full descriptor. Allocate includes its delimiter
once. Position sender, destination and length by the recorded Base/Seek
or actual delimiter scans. The six-row copy of Proposition 6.3 then
performs each stage, including its final length test, seal and TOK. The
second stage begins only after the mailbox seal and authorized handoff.
Its sender and counter are repositioned by those same paid scans.
The mailbox's idle writer/availability token is transferred to this
channel setup by an actual TOK and returned after acknowledgement by an
actual TOK; these are additional to the two copy-body handoffs. The
receiver's installed client writer token authorizes its own setup rows.
Client receiver blocks append after its old cells; none aliases the
mailbox, service archive or source. Their actual creation chain and the
payload's version/seed/cover binding are retained with the delivery.
Only the final receiver seal enables its read token. This version then
always serially prints that received result, with no further external
choice. After the completion record, recover the receiver base from its
staged placement record and Seek(40); recopy the sender's actual payload
length to 35 and position that counter. The print loop is RD(35,5);
on a leading 1, RD(40,4); OUT(4); MR(40); MR(35), then repeat; on 0 exit.
Its complete tested body is $5l+1$, of which $3l$ is RD/OUT/data-head work.
Length recovery, both positionings, public read authority, acknowledgement
and final return remain additional paid rows. No free access to a bank
or unsealed receiver is used. Independent extra client reads or optional
printing choices are outside this fixed canonical-frame graph; adding
such choices would require their own retained control-input relation
before claiming replay closure for them.

The service availability token is a finite token distinct from source
ownership. At the installed Ready it belongs to $C$. A request begins
with its actual TOK to the handler and reserve CTL; busy or missing-token
attempts are illegal and unexecuted. After full outcome delivery, client acknowledgement and the common
frame-return bank updates, that common return performs the TOK to $C$,
followed by the Ready CTL. Publish itself never executes a Ready return.
The fixed client may keep its received immutable packages and read tokens;
repeated calls use that same installed token relation. A numeric $p$ alone
never grants it. Unknown $p$ with the token yields executed NoPackage;
known $p$ without the token yields no transition. A returned historical
token bit never executes this live TOK. Installation pays for the token's
finite encoded initial state and its first handoff. More clients or token
minting are not claimed by this one-client construction.

